---
import Article from "../../../layouts/Article.astro";
import KeyTakeaway from "../../../components/KeyTakeaway.astro";
import InfoBox from "../../../components/InfoBox.astro";
import StepList from "../../../components/StepList.astro";
import Step from "../../../components/Step.astro";
import QuickFact from "../../../components/QuickFact.astro";
---

<Article
  title="How to Play Agricola: Complete Rules for New Players"
  description="Fourteen rounds, six harvests, and one question that never goes away — can you feed everyone?"
  game="agricola"
  gameTitle="Agricola"
  category="Rules"
  product="agricola-resource-tokens"
  publishDate="2026-08-09"
>
  <p>
    Agricola is the game that made worker placement a genre. It's also famous for
    being cruel: you start with two family members, a two-room wooden hut, and a
    farm that produces nothing. Everything you need has to be taken from a board
    that everyone else is grabbing from too.
  </p>

  <KeyTakeaway answer="Place your family members on action spaces to gather resources, build your farm and grow your family. Every few rounds a harvest arrives and demands two food per adult. Score by having a bit of everything at the end." />

  <div class="my-8 flex flex-wrap gap-3">
    <QuickFact label="Players" value="1–4" icon="👥" />
    <QuickFact label="Rounds" value="14" icon="🔁" />
    <QuickFact label="Harvests" value="6" icon="🌾" />
    <QuickFact label="Weight" value="Heavy" icon="🧠" />
  </div>

  <h2>The Core Loop</h2>

  <p>
    Each of the fourteen rounds follows the same rhythm, and it is worth learning
    this shape before anything else.
  </p>

  <StepList title="A round of Agricola">
    <Step number={1} title="Reveal a new action space">
      A new card is turned face up, adding a new action to the board. The board
      literally grows as the game goes on — early rounds have very few options.
    </Step>
    <Step number={2} title="Replenish the accumulation spaces">
      Certain spaces gather resources over time. If nobody took the wood last
      round, there's now more wood sitting there.
    </Step>
    <Step number={3} title="Place family members, one at a time">
      In turn order, each player puts one family member on an empty action space
      and takes that action immediately. Continue until everyone has placed all
      their people.
    </Step>
    <Step number={4} title="Return home">
      All family members come back. If this round ends with a harvest, resolve it
      now.
    </Step>
  </StepList>

  <InfoBox type="rule" title="One worker per space, always">
    Every action space holds exactly one family member for the round. If someone
    takes the space you needed, it is gone until next round. This is the entire
    source of tension in Agricola.
  </InfoBox>

  <h2>What You Can Do</h2>

  <p>
    The available actions grow through the game, but they fall into a few
    recognisable groups:
  </p>

  <div class="my-6 grid sm:grid-cols-2 gap-4">
    <div class="p-5 bg-white rounded-xl border border-bark/10">
      <h4 class="font-heading font-semibold text-sm text-bark mb-3">🪵 Gathering</h4>
      <ul class="text-sm text-bark/60 space-y-1">
        <li>• Wood, clay, reed and stone</li>
        <li>• Grain and vegetable seeds</li>
        <li>• Food, directly (usually inefficiently)</li>
        <li>• Sheep, wild boar and cattle</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div class="p-5 bg-white rounded-xl border border-bark/10">
      <h4 class="font-heading font-semibold text-sm text-bark mb-3">🏠 Developing</h4>
      <ul class="text-sm text-bark/60 space-y-1">
        <li>• Plough a field</li>
        <li>• Sow grain or vegetables</li>
        <li>• Build rooms, fences and stables</li>
        <li>• Grow your family</li>
        <li>• Renovate your house</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2>The Harvest</h2>

  <p>
    Six times across the game, a harvest interrupts everything. It has three
    phases, in order:
  </p>

  <StepList title="Resolving a harvest">
    <Step number={1} title="Field phase">
      Take one grain or vegetable from each sown field. Fields don't produce
      forever — each planted crop yields for a limited number of harvests.
    </Step>
    <Step number={2} title="Feeding phase">
      Pay <strong>2 food</strong> for every adult family member and
      <strong>1 food</strong> for anyone born since the last harvest. If you can't
      pay, you take a <strong>begging card</strong> for each missing food — each
      worth <strong>−3 points</strong> at the end.
    </Step>
    <Step number={3} title="Breeding phase">
      If you have at least two of an animal type, you gain one more — provided you
      have somewhere to put it.
    </Step>
  </StepList>

  <InfoBox type="warning" title="Grain is not food">
    Raw grain, vegetables and animals do not feed anyone. You need a Fireplace,
    Cooking Hearth or similar improvement to convert them, and if you never build
    one you will be scraping food off the board by hand all game. This is the
    single most common beginner disaster — see
    <a href="/games/agricola/agricola-feeding-your-family/">the feeding guide</a>.
  </InfoBox>

  <h2>Growing Your Family</h2>

  <p>
    You begin with two family members, which means two actions per round. Growing
    your family gives you a third, then a fourth, then a fifth — more actions than
    anyone else, every single round for the rest of the game.
  </p>

  <p>
    The catch is that each new person needs a <strong>room to live in</strong>
    before they can be born, and each one raises your food bill by 2 every
    harvest. Family growth is simultaneously the strongest move in the game and
    the fastest route to a pile of begging cards.
  </p>

  <h2>Scoring</h2>

  <p>
    Agricola scores you on breadth, not depth. You get points for each category
    based on how much you have — and crucially, you get <strong>negative
    points</strong> for having none of something.
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Fields, pastures and fenced stables</strong> — more is better</li>
    <li><strong>Grain and vegetables</strong> — counted at game end</li>
    <li><strong>Sheep, wild boar and cattle</strong> — each type scored separately</li>
    <li><strong>Rooms and house type</strong> — clay and stone rooms score more than wood</li>
    <li><strong>Family members</strong> — a solid 3 points each</li>
    <li><strong>Unused farmyard spaces</strong> — <strong>−1 each</strong></li>
    <li><strong>Begging cards</strong> — <strong>−3 each</strong></li>
  </ul>

  <InfoBox type="tip" title="Zero in a category is a real penalty">
    Having no sheep at all is worse than having one sheep, because empty
    categories score negative. A single animal of each type, a couple of
    vegetables and a ploughed field will quietly out-score a beautiful farm that
    specialised in one thing.
  </InfoBox>

  <h2>Which Edition Are You Playing?</h2>

  <p>
    The full game includes Occupation and Minor Improvement cards dealt to each
    player, which add enormous variety and complexity. Most editions also include
    a <strong>family game</strong> variant that removes them entirely.
  </p>

  <p>
    Play the family variant first. The core farm-building puzzle is hard enough on
    its own, and adding fourteen unfamiliar cards to your first game turns a tough
    teach into an unpleasant one.
  </p>

  <p>
    Where to spend those precious early actions is covered in
    <a href="/games/agricola/agricola-beginner-strategy/">your first five rounds</a>.
  </p>
</Article>
